1
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,360
In the first lecture,
that be covered

2
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:09,000
is that large
language models are

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,880
really good at solving natural
language processing problems,

4
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,520
and I think most of you are
very familiar with using them

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,700
in chatbot scenarios.

6
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,940
But to make them useful
in real-world tasks,

7
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:21,400
they do need to be
able to interact

8
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,600
with real-world environments,
tools, and code,

9
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:28,560
and learn from what
they interact with.

10
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:32,360
So you already saw some
examples of test-time compute

11
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,000
and robust verification that
Azalea covered in the last two

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:35,900
lectures.

13
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,960
Now, we'll build a little
bit more on top of that.

14
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,400
So today's lecture, we will
focus on three different papers.

15
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:46,460
The first paper will start with
the notion of tool calling,

16
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:50,560
and how can we get the models
to go beyond just reasoning

17
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:53,942
to also take into account
actions via tool calls?

18
00:00:53,942 --> 00:00:55,400
And the paper that
we'll cover here

19
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:58,120
is called ReAct, which
actually was one of the first

20
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:01,460
that went around to combine
reasoning and acting in language

21
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:03,060
models.

22
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:05,060
The second paper
that we'll look into

23
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:09,840
is RLEF or grounding code
LLMs in execution feedback.

24
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:13,980
What this will focus on
is how do you actually

25
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:17,900
do code generation while using
execution feedback from being

26
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,860
able to run the code or
run the tests of the code.

27
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:22,940
And the third thing
that we'll look into

28
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:27,740
is constitutional AI, where we
use the model critiquing itself

29
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:29,960
as a feedback loop
to improve the model.

30
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:32,860
So all three of those techniques
are ways for the model

31
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:34,060
to improve itself.

32
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:36,300
But what differs in
each of these techniques

33
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:38,660
is, where is the
feedback coming from?

34
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:41,340
It's some form of interaction
with either the environment

35
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:44,060
or some form of critique
mechanism or grounding

36
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:48,180
using what we know should
be the right answer.

37
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:56,220
So let's start with tool calling
as the first abstraction.

38
00:01:56,220 --> 00:02:00,280
So what ReAct really
tried to focus on

39
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:03,820
was this notion of when humans
want to go and do some action,

40
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:05,260
they think about it first.

41
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:09,060
For example, if you decide that
you want to come take a class,

42
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:13,760
you think about how that
might actually help you.

43
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:17,640
And what this roughly
means is that, once you

44
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:20,940
have thought about it, then
you will have a reason to act.

45
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:22,560
And then based on
that action, you

46
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:24,943
might observe that you
have a new observation

47
00:02:24,943 --> 00:02:25,860
about the environment.

48
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:27,160
So once you come
to the class, you

49
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:29,660
might decide you like the class
or you don't like the class.

50
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:33,020
And based on that information,
you come up with new reasoning.

51
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:35,040
Maybe you'll go do the
homework, and then that

52
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:36,860
will make you decide
something else.

53
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:39,280
So there is this loop
between thinking and action

54
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,000
that helps us
function as humans.

55
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,120
A similar loop can be very
useful in terms of refining

56
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:48,960
what language models generate.

57
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:52,560
And typically, when you
look at language models,

58
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:55,120
they have struggled to
combine reasoning and acting

59
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:57,860
by themselves as
isolated processes.

60
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:00,242
In today's models, the
reasoning has become innate

61
00:03:00,242 --> 00:03:02,200
because thinking has
become part of the models.

62
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,940
But if you look at the
history of the LLMs

63
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:06,700
in the last couple
of years, this

64
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:09,180
was not something that
came out of the box.

65
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:12,100
And one of the challenges of
why it was hard for models

66
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:15,940
to combine reasoning and acting
is because they typically

67
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:16,960
will hallucinate.

68
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:18,948
They will have no context
of what is actually

69
00:03:18,948 --> 00:03:20,240
happening in their environment.

70
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:21,900
So, for example,
if you were to type

71
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:24,780
into a model like
Gemini or ChatGPT

72
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:30,040
or Claude, what is
the temperature today?

73
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:31,820
Where is something
happening today?

74
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:34,740
It has no information
about the current events,

75
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:37,180
unless it actually goes
and does a search call

76
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:39,580
and then fetches
that information.

77
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:42,780
So it has no notion of
grounding in the real world.

78
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:46,660
And as a result of that, it
cannot go and take actions,

79
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:49,800
and then actually combine
that back into reasoning.

80
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,620
So ReAct was one of
the first abstractions

81
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:54,800
that tried to combine it in a
way that would be effective.

82
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:57,000
And what you see
today's models do

83
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,440
is actually go beyond ReAct to
do this in the chain of thought

84
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:02,640
itself.

85
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:05,760
So let's take a look at what
we covered in lecture 1.

86
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:08,820
So if you look at chain
of thought by itself,

87
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:10,560
chain of thought
already gives the model

88
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:11,620
some sense of reasoning.

89
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:13,380
So if you gave it
a math problem,

90
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:16,079
the model will show the steps,
the intermediate steps, it

91
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:19,240
takes to get to the answer,
but these steps may or may not

92
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:22,740
be grounded because they are
based on models internal state.

93
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:25,080
They have no feedback
from the external world.

94
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:27,240
And then there is a
second class of LLMs

95
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,440
that were developed,
for example, WebGPT,

96
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:31,822
which go and interact
with, say, web browsers.

97
00:04:31,822 --> 00:04:33,280
And what they're
basically learning

98
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:36,320
is how to interact with
web browsers, which

99
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:37,680
is a form of a tool.

100
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:40,100
So they don't necessarily
have a reasoning process,

101
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:41,680
but they're basically
learning how

102
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:43,820
to interact with web browsers.

103
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:46,240
So you basically can
fine-tune on traces

104
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:49,060
that are interaction
traces with web browsers,

105
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:51,460
and then what do humans prefer?

106
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:54,193
But as a result of those
two different things,

107
00:04:54,193 --> 00:04:55,860
one is focused more
on reasoning and one

108
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:58,220
is focused more on actions.

109
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:00,020
How do we combine
these two paradigms

110
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:03,900
where we can both reason
and act in a useful way?

111
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:06,140
A very simple abstraction
that ReAct came up with

112
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:08,380
was just using prompting.

113
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:10,540
You can get the model to
generate verbal reasoning

114
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:11,400
traces.

115
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:15,100
So first, you say, OK, I want
the model to go do this task

116
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:18,600
and ask the model to think,
say, step by step about it,

117
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:20,760
and then it comes back
with its reasoning trace.

118
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:23,020
You append that in the
prompt, and then you

119
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:25,600
say, OK, what action should
be taken based on that?

120
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:27,860
And then you go take an action.

121
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:30,420
This really helps the
model improve performance

122
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:32,040
where grounding matters.

123
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:34,920
So HotpotQA, for example, is
a question-answering task.

124
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:36,680
Then FEVER is a
fact-checking task.

125
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:38,600
So these require
real-world knowledge.

126
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:41,540
They require knowledge about
what is happening in the world.

127
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:44,220
And then WebShop, for
example, is a task

128
00:05:44,220 --> 00:05:46,860
where you're supposed
to ask the model to go

129
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:48,360
buy products on the internet.

130
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:50,900
So it's supposed to do
a series of tool calls

131
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:53,975
to actually identify
what product to search,

132
00:05:53,975 --> 00:05:55,600
and then have sequence
of those actions

133
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:58,360
to actually complete the
purchase for the user.

134
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:01,080
So each of these tasks
require some notion

135
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:02,460
of grounding in the real world.

136
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:04,880
And without that
grounding, it's not

137
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:07,280
possible for the model
to keep making progress.

138
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:09,680
A second benefit
of having the model

139
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:12,160
explicitly do
reasoning and action

140
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:15,400
steps is that it
will allow the model

141
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:17,680
to give traces that
are more interpretable,

142
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:21,480
and interpretable allows
humans to really trust

143
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:22,540
the model's responses.

144
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:25,080
It's almost like getting
the model to break down

145
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:28,040
what it's doing step by step.

146
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:30,000
So what you might be
familiar with is that,

147
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,680
if you prompt the LLM and
you ask it to think step

148
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:36,570
by step, which we were
showing in earlier examples,

149
00:06:36,570 --> 00:06:38,320
then you will get some
form of a reasoning

150
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,980
trace where it shows
its intermediate steps.

151
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:43,280
And then, if you ask the
model to, say, do a tool,

152
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:48,820
call go search, for example,
Google or find the temperature,

153
00:06:48,820 --> 00:06:50,360
it will go call an
API, and it will

154
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:51,640
get a bunch of observations.

155
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:54,070
So that would be action only.

156
00:06:54,070 --> 00:06:55,820
Typically, you will
have a list of actions

157
00:06:55,820 --> 00:07:00,420
if you have a list of tool
calls that you are valid.

158
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:03,460
What ReAct does is
it will take a goal

159
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:06,752
and have some form of reasoning,
so that will be the left loop.

160
00:07:06,752 --> 00:07:08,460
And then based on
those reasoning chains,

161
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:09,640
it will go and act.

162
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:11,320
That will be the
loop in the right.

163
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:18,360
So what this is setting up
is a full agent in itself,

164
00:07:18,360 --> 00:07:20,820
where you have some
observations about the world

165
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:23,760
and then some set of actions
based on what's the context.

166
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:26,820
And if you were to, for example,
take this entire context

167
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:29,580
with observations and the set
of actions that have happened

168
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:32,400
in the past, typically, you
could go learn a policy,

169
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:35,100
but that would require
a full loop, which

170
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:36,700
will be complex and expensive.

171
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:39,380
But you can actually start doing
this in language space, which

172
00:07:39,380 --> 00:07:42,860
is what ReAct is doing, where
it's basically just generating

173
00:07:42,860 --> 00:07:45,312
the thoughts in
the language space,

174
00:07:45,312 --> 00:07:47,520
and those thoughts are not
affecting the environment.

175
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:48,640
And then based on
those thoughts,

176
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:50,560
it's then going and
taking an action, just

177
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:51,620
in the prompting space.

178
00:07:51,620 --> 00:07:53,240
So it's a very
simple abstraction

179
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,080
of how to prompt
the models to start

180
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:59,800
thinking about these
things and generate

181
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:01,280
actions, which are valid.

182
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:03,160
One interesting concept
I'll mention here

183
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:06,640
is that, when you get the
models to generate actions

184
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:09,120
in this particular
slide, typically,

185
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:11,200
how do you know that
these actions are valid?

186
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:13,400
So one typical way
in which you get

187
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:20,040
these models to generate correct
actions is to give them a more--

188
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:22,340
to frame the problem more
as a classification task.

189
00:08:22,340 --> 00:08:25,180
So you say that this is the
valid subset of actions,

190
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:28,253
and then use that as a way to
say, OK, here is your reasoning.

191
00:08:28,253 --> 00:08:29,920
Here is the state of
the world, and here

192
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:32,580
is-- what are the valid set
of actions to go take next?

193
00:08:32,580 --> 00:08:34,419
And then based on that,
it goes and decides,

194
00:08:34,419 --> 00:08:36,480
OK, this is the action
to take, and then

195
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:39,100
that generally provides
a more grounded response.

196
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:42,360
And in fact, this was
also used in another paper

197
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,680
that we worked on in
collaboration at Google

198
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:48,220
with robotics folks, where
the state of the world and all

199
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:50,280
of that requires
very valid actions.

200
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:52,900
So you can't really
operate without having

201
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:53,760
a list of actions.

202
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:56,180
So that provides one way to
really grounded knowledge

203
00:08:56,180 --> 00:08:59,460
and what actions to take next.

204
00:08:59,460 --> 00:09:05,140
So in terms of implementing
this idea in a very simple way,

205
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:07,620
this was done with--

206
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:09,780
this is an older model,
but with the PaLM model

207
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:11,460
with a frozen LLM.

208
00:09:11,460 --> 00:09:13,380
In few-shot examples,
the model was

209
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:15,980
given some actions, some
thoughts, and some observations,

210
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:18,120
and then asked to go
take the next action.

211
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:20,300
So for the reasoning
traces, it was basically

212
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:22,120
seeing actions and thoughts.

213
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:23,480
I'll show you an example.

214
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:26,820
And for the decision tasks,
it was basically looking at,

215
00:09:26,820 --> 00:09:28,280
what's the state of the world?

216
00:09:28,280 --> 00:09:29,400
What is the thinking?

217
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:31,460
And then deciding
whether to reason

218
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:33,280
and whether to take action next.

219
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:34,960
So you can either go
and reason, or you

220
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:38,820
can decide whether I have to
reason or take action next.

221
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:43,300
So let's look through an example
and walk through it carefully.

222
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:48,240
So aside from the Apple
remote, what other device

223
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:51,560
can control the
program Apple remote

224
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:53,820
was originally designed
to interact with?

225
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:57,040
So this is basically a
question from HotpotQA.

226
00:09:57,040 --> 00:09:59,480
If you basically just
do standard prompting,

227
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:02,233
the answer that will come
back will not be correct.

228
00:10:02,233 --> 00:10:04,400
Now, you can do Chain of
Thought where you basically

229
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:06,260
ask the model to go
think step by step.

230
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:08,840
It's showing you all the
intermediate steps here.

231
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:11,720
However, the answer is
still not coming out

232
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:13,060
to be completely correct.

233
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:16,360
Now, let's see.

234
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:18,400
If we ask it to take
some actions where

235
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:20,120
it can do some
grounded knowledge,

236
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:23,220
and it can do some search
calls, so it goes and sees that,

237
00:10:23,220 --> 00:10:26,600
OK, the thing that I'm really
needing more information on

238
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:27,960
is Apple remote.

239
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:30,820
So it goes and does a
action on Apple remote.

240
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:32,420
It gets some observation.

241
00:10:32,420 --> 00:10:35,040
Then it goes and does
another search call

242
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:40,080
on Front Row, which is some text
that is there in observation 1

243
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:42,540
and then continues to follow on.

244
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:45,380
And then it realizes
that, OK, this

245
00:10:45,380 --> 00:10:46,840
is a discontinued something.

246
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:49,060
So as a result, there is
not really a good answer

247
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:51,980
that it can give.

248
00:10:51,980 --> 00:10:56,500
Now, how does thinking and
action interleaving those traces

249
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:57,000
help?

250
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,020
So here, it thinks
first that, OK,

251
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:00,580
I have to search
for Apple remote,

252
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:02,860
and then find the
program it was originally

253
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:04,120
designed to interact with.

254
00:11:04,120 --> 00:11:06,300
So it first goes and
searches Apple remote.

255
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:08,220
It finds the
information that-- it's

256
00:11:08,220 --> 00:11:13,260
a remote control introduced very
early on, 2005, two decades ago.

257
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:16,900
And it was originally designed
to control the Front Row media

258
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:17,700
center.

259
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:21,560
Then it thinks, OK, so I need
to search Front Row next.

260
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:23,480
So it goes and
searches for this.

261
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:25,120
It's not able to find it.

262
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:27,260
So then it comes up
with a new terminology

263
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:28,442
called Front Row software.

264
00:11:28,442 --> 00:11:29,900
And then it searches
that, and it's

265
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:32,540
able to figure out, OK, it's
a discontinued media software

266
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:33,580
center.

267
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:36,100
And then it thinks
about it again,

268
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:39,180
so it's able to give an
answer based on that.

269
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:41,580
So basically, the
reasoning process

270
00:11:41,580 --> 00:11:43,920
is an abstraction, which
allows it to come back

271
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:46,380
with better answer than
just taking actions

272
00:11:46,380 --> 00:11:48,520
and then coming back
with answers in between.

273
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:52,160
And interleaving the thought
and action and the observation

274
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:55,560
aspect is basically allowing
the model to behave better.

275
00:11:55,560 --> 00:11:57,560
Now, what is
happening in the LLMs

276
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:00,160
today where, for example,
if you go in the thinking

277
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:03,180
mode of, say, Qwen
or other models,

278
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:05,060
you will see this
happening automatically.

279
00:12:05,060 --> 00:12:06,893
So if you actually turn
on the thinking mode

280
00:12:06,893 --> 00:12:08,320
and say, Qwen
models, you will see

281
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:12,160
all of this starting to happen
because the models have already

282
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:13,900
been distilled on these traces.

283
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:15,520
So the thinking
fusion stuff that

284
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:17,720
happens in several of
the open-source models

285
00:12:17,720 --> 00:12:19,238
already captures these things.

286
00:12:19,238 --> 00:12:20,780
So they've already
learned how to do,

287
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:23,240
for example, tool
calls and so on.

288
00:12:23,240 --> 00:12:24,000
Questions?

289
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:28,640
Yes.

290
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:33,840
How does the model know what
it knows, what to search,

291
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:35,600
or when to search?

292
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:38,280
Because clearly, you don't
want to search on 1 plus 1.

293
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:40,800
But how do you
determine the search?

294
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:42,260
How do you determine--

295
00:12:42,260 --> 00:12:43,980
I mean, here, it's
searching, and then

296
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:47,880
it's basically coming up with--
here, it's getting a feedback.

297
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:49,340
It doesn't know what it knows.

298
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:51,940
This is from interaction.

299
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:55,140
But you already have been
trained on Front Row Wikipedia

300
00:12:55,140 --> 00:12:55,900
page.

301
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:57,120
You shouldn't need to search.

302
00:12:57,120 --> 00:12:58,450
Yes, It doesn't need to search.

303
00:12:58,450 --> 00:12:58,950
Yes.

304
00:12:58,950 --> 00:13:01,740
But does the model know
whether it knows this?

305
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:04,580
So I think there's
contradictory set

306
00:13:04,580 --> 00:13:07,120
of opinions on whether the
model knows what it knows.

307
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:09,620
There is one set of
people that will tell you

308
00:13:09,620 --> 00:13:13,740
that models are very confident
in knowing what they know.

309
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:15,760
But I think the other
side is that, typically,

310
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:18,940
if you were to ask the
model to rate its output of,

311
00:13:18,940 --> 00:13:20,720
are you confident
about an output?

312
00:13:20,720 --> 00:13:23,580
Typically, the models
are overconfident.

313
00:13:23,580 --> 00:13:25,540
They're not well-calibrated.

314
00:13:25,540 --> 00:13:28,980
I mean that's a research problem
that is still being solved.

315
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:34,500
So as someone who's
designing a application, what

316
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:36,625
you are looking
for is not so much

317
00:13:36,625 --> 00:13:38,500
knowing whether the
model knows what to know,

318
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:41,280
but getting the model to
use the right set of tools

319
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:43,760
so that you have
grounded knowledge.

320
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:44,780
Does that make sense?

321
00:13:44,780 --> 00:13:45,440
Yes.

322
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:46,960
Yes.

323
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:49,920
With the ReAct on
the right, when

324
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:51,840
you're saying with
the left loop,

325
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:55,740
you elicit the reasoning to then
append to original knowledge.

326
00:13:55,740 --> 00:13:58,400
Will it produce the
reasoning of the one or two

327
00:13:58,400 --> 00:14:03,280
or three or four initially,
and then it goes and just acts

328
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:06,120
for each one, or does the act
influence the next reasoning

329
00:14:06,120 --> 00:14:07,600
step?

330
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:11,340
It's interleaved, so it's
thought one, then act one.

331
00:14:11,340 --> 00:14:13,760
So it's happening in an
interleaved fashion--

332
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:17,380
thought one, act one,
thought two, act two.

333
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:20,880
So it's basically each
one is informing the next.

334
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:24,800
It's very much like
the human process.

335
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:27,003
You're thirsty,
so you, basically,

336
00:14:27,003 --> 00:14:28,420
start walking
towards the kitchen.

337
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:30,560
You get to the kitchen,
maybe water is out.

338
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:33,260
So then you decide the
next step based on that.

339
00:14:33,260 --> 00:14:35,880
And so you think that maybe I
have to go to the supermarket

340
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:36,740
to go do something.

341
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:40,380
So it's very sequential
in that respect.

342
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:43,060
You can do parallel sampling
and all of those tricks,

343
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:46,480
but then you have to
explicitly design that process.

344
00:14:49,420 --> 00:14:49,920
Yes.

345
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:52,660
There's no guarantees as a
search result. [INAUDIBLE].

346
00:14:52,660 --> 00:14:56,940
So what do you [INAUDIBLE]
observe [INAUDIBLE]

347
00:14:56,940 --> 00:15:00,020
is the search result?

348
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:01,700
That's a great question.

349
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:04,940
If there's contradiction
between the results,

350
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:08,700
then what do you want the--

351
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:10,940
I think this is a
question that the user has

352
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:15,220
to decide if there's
not enough consensus.

353
00:15:15,220 --> 00:15:18,180
So typically, one of the things
that we covered in second class

354
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:21,860
was that we can
get more confidence

355
00:15:21,860 --> 00:15:24,100
by doing, say, majority voting.

356
00:15:24,100 --> 00:15:27,020
So you basically
say, OK, or you have

357
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:28,540
some way of
validating the answer

358
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:29,960
before you give it to the user.

359
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:32,140
So for the end application,
what you care about is

360
00:15:32,140 --> 00:15:33,900
the answer is valid.

361
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:35,520
So you need more guardrails.

362
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:41,040
But hallucination
with search results

363
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:43,240
can be controlled
much better than just

364
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:47,080
depending on the internal
state of the model.

365
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:49,920
Other questions?

366
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:50,940
Yes.

367
00:15:50,940 --> 00:15:54,440
Maybe this is not answerable,
but also [INAUDIBLE] from the--

368
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:57,920
I wonder if you have looked
at the KV-cache representation

369
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:01,160
of the thought versus
the observation

370
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:04,160
and see if there is any sort
of interleaving patterns

371
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:07,000
between them, given that the
observations are generally

372
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:08,200
given by the environment.

373
00:16:08,200 --> 00:16:10,600
They are not generated
by the LLM itself.

374
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:15,200
So I wonder if there would
be these changes in between.

375
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:18,360
That might be your research
project for this class.

376
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:20,980
There are open-source LLMs
that you can work with.

377
00:16:24,040 --> 00:16:25,680
Yes.

378
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:29,200
It's just a little termination,
like technology [INAUDIBLE],

379
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:32,960
but I know this is like
the action space of ReAct

380
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:36,820
from where it's the actions
plus thought source.

381
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:40,380
Why do we just think
of thoughts internal

382
00:16:40,380 --> 00:16:43,620
to the policy model,
and maybe the action

383
00:16:43,620 --> 00:16:47,220
is just like search or finish?

384
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:51,020
Why do we say source is
also part of action space?

385
00:16:51,020 --> 00:16:53,040
We're not calling it
part of action space.

386
00:16:53,040 --> 00:16:55,820
We are calling it
a separate thing.

387
00:16:55,820 --> 00:16:58,300
It's like actions are
on the right loop,

388
00:16:58,300 --> 00:17:01,060
and the reasoning traces
are on the left loop.

389
00:17:01,060 --> 00:17:06,619
Are you talking about this one?

390
00:17:06,619 --> 00:17:10,140
So I think because the language
models are trained on language,

391
00:17:10,140 --> 00:17:12,940
typically, they benefit from
having the reasoning tokens

392
00:17:12,940 --> 00:17:19,119
in the right abstraction as a
way to output the right action.

393
00:17:19,119 --> 00:17:21,440
So that's the main reason
to have those tokens.

394
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:23,579
I mean, if you had
intermediate representations,

395
00:17:23,579 --> 00:17:24,800
then maybe it doesn't matter.

396
00:17:28,580 --> 00:17:30,480
So let's take a look
at some results.

397
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:31,820
So these two tasks--

398
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:35,160
HotpotQA is a multi-hop
question-answering task

399
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:39,000
over Wikipedia, and then FEVER
is more of a fact-checking task.

400
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,600
The action space
in these two tasks

401
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:44,900
are very much focused on, say,
search over Wikipedia pages

402
00:17:44,900 --> 00:17:47,520
or looking up a certain
string or finishing

403
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:50,180
that, OK, I finished
whatever task I was given.

404
00:17:50,180 --> 00:17:51,980
The action space is
actually very simple.

405
00:17:51,980 --> 00:17:56,160
It's basically simulating the
human interaction with Wikipedia

406
00:17:56,160 --> 00:17:57,800
here.

407
00:17:57,800 --> 00:17:59,352
So the baselines
here are simple,

408
00:17:59,352 --> 00:18:01,060
where you're basically
building a prompt.

409
00:18:01,060 --> 00:18:03,480
The standard prompting
that you will

410
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:06,620
see as simple prompting,
no thoughts, no actions,

411
00:18:06,620 --> 00:18:07,547
no observations.

412
00:18:07,547 --> 00:18:09,880
Chain of thought where you're
basically asking the model

413
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:11,440
to think step by step.

414
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:13,540
Chain of Thought,
self-consistency--

415
00:18:13,540 --> 00:18:15,160
SC stands for
self-consistency-- where

416
00:18:15,160 --> 00:18:18,060
you're basically doing majority
voting or self-consistency.

417
00:18:18,060 --> 00:18:20,640
Both of those terminologies
are exactly the same thing.

418
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:23,940
And then Act refers to there
is no thinking in between.

419
00:18:23,940 --> 00:18:25,960
You're basically
just taking actions.

420
00:18:25,960 --> 00:18:28,680
And there is two variants
you will see in the table

421
00:18:28,680 --> 00:18:31,040
where you can fall back
to Chain of Thought,

422
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:34,010
self-consistency if ReAct
fails after a certain steps,

423
00:18:34,010 --> 00:18:35,190
and then this.

424
00:18:35,190 --> 00:18:37,810
If the majority answer occurs
less than half the time,

425
00:18:37,810 --> 00:18:39,290
then it can back off to ReAct.

426
00:18:39,290 --> 00:18:41,790
So you'll see this
in the results.

427
00:18:41,790 --> 00:18:46,450
So this is an example of when
they prompted this large model.

428
00:18:46,450 --> 00:18:49,190
What they found was
that ReAct generally

429
00:18:49,190 --> 00:18:51,230
does better than action only.

430
00:18:51,230 --> 00:18:53,950
It did not always
outperform Chain of Thought,

431
00:18:53,950 --> 00:18:55,550
which was interesting.

432
00:18:55,550 --> 00:18:58,250
It outperformed chain of
thought in the case of FEVER.

433
00:18:58,250 --> 00:19:01,730
But in case of HotpotQA, it
did not always outperform.

434
00:19:01,730 --> 00:19:03,830
But if you combined
this Chain of Thought,

435
00:19:03,830 --> 00:19:07,270
self-consistency with either
fallback or the other way

436
00:19:07,270 --> 00:19:09,910
around, then you do
actually outperform

437
00:19:09,910 --> 00:19:11,215
the standard techniques.

438
00:19:11,215 --> 00:19:12,590
So what that
roughly tells you is

439
00:19:12,590 --> 00:19:14,630
that there is value
in properly combining

440
00:19:14,630 --> 00:19:17,250
the model internal knowledge
with the external knowledge,

441
00:19:17,250 --> 00:19:20,950
and then using reasoning as an
intermediate way for the model

442
00:19:20,950 --> 00:19:22,650
to arrive at the right answer.

443
00:19:22,650 --> 00:19:24,230
So basically, that
allows the model

444
00:19:24,230 --> 00:19:28,710
to reason what is the right
and accurate information

445
00:19:28,710 --> 00:19:31,070
to retrieve here.

446
00:19:31,070 --> 00:19:32,570
And it's basically
showing that it's

447
00:19:32,570 --> 00:19:38,130
able to use search as a tool to
retrieve the right information.

448
00:19:38,130 --> 00:19:41,570
And then another interesting
aspect here is that--

449
00:19:41,570 --> 00:19:44,230
what this is saying is,
when it's successful,

450
00:19:44,230 --> 00:19:45,323
what is happening?

451
00:19:45,323 --> 00:19:46,990
Did it get the correct
reasoning traces?

452
00:19:46,990 --> 00:19:50,070
And when it's failing,
what errors are happening?

453
00:19:50,070 --> 00:19:52,630
So one of the things that I
want to highlight in this slide

454
00:19:52,630 --> 00:19:54,290
without going through
too much detail

455
00:19:54,290 --> 00:19:56,690
is that, in Chain of
Thought, hallucination

456
00:19:56,690 --> 00:19:59,670
ends up being a major
problem, a major failure mode.

457
00:19:59,670 --> 00:20:03,715
But in ReAct, you basically end
up having grounded information,

458
00:20:03,715 --> 00:20:05,090
and the model is
more trustworthy

459
00:20:05,090 --> 00:20:07,550
because it has access to
this external knowledge base.

460
00:20:07,550 --> 00:20:10,530
So it allows it to
make fewer errors

461
00:20:10,530 --> 00:20:12,690
because it can
successfully retrieve

462
00:20:12,690 --> 00:20:15,475
the knowledge via search.

463
00:20:15,475 --> 00:20:16,850
And then there is
some difference

464
00:20:16,850 --> 00:20:18,653
between prompting
versus fine-tuning.

465
00:20:18,653 --> 00:20:21,070
When you can fine-tune, then
ReAct definitely does better.

466
00:20:21,070 --> 00:20:22,528
And then, if you
can do outer loop,

467
00:20:22,528 --> 00:20:27,150
then it does even better,
which we'll cover next week.

468
00:20:27,150 --> 00:20:30,050
So then that was
knowledge-intensive tasks.

469
00:20:30,050 --> 00:20:32,110
If you were to look at
decision-making tasks--

470
00:20:32,110 --> 00:20:35,990
and this can be, say, online
web browser experiments

471
00:20:35,990 --> 00:20:39,670
or, say, robotics experiments
or gaming environments,

472
00:20:39,670 --> 00:20:43,210
where we will look at
WebShop as an example--

473
00:20:43,210 --> 00:20:45,070
you require the agent
to purchase a product

474
00:20:45,070 --> 00:20:46,450
based on user instructions.

475
00:20:46,450 --> 00:20:48,590
For example, you're
asking the model

476
00:20:48,590 --> 00:20:51,050
to look for a
nightstand with drawers,

477
00:20:51,050 --> 00:20:52,630
and then you're
basically evaluating

478
00:20:52,630 --> 00:20:55,657
whether the model gets to
success when it's interacting

479
00:20:55,657 --> 00:20:56,490
in this environment.

480
00:20:56,490 --> 00:20:58,050
It's a simulated environment.

481
00:20:58,050 --> 00:21:00,770
And then you compare it as a
baseline to imitation learning.

482
00:21:00,770 --> 00:21:02,130
That's just fine-tuning.

483
00:21:02,130 --> 00:21:03,730
And then imitation
learning plus RL,

484
00:21:03,730 --> 00:21:07,030
which is basically just
supervised fine-tuning plus RL.

485
00:21:07,030 --> 00:21:08,630
And the results
that you see here

486
00:21:08,630 --> 00:21:10,788
are impressive in the
sense that, even compared

487
00:21:10,788 --> 00:21:12,830
to, say, imitation learning
or imitation learning

488
00:21:12,830 --> 00:21:17,070
plus RL, having the reasoning
abstraction with ReAct,

489
00:21:17,070 --> 00:21:19,670
you get higher score, and
you get higher success rates

490
00:21:19,670 --> 00:21:22,030
compared to what's
the baseline here.

491
00:21:22,030 --> 00:21:25,370
So the score refers
to immediate steps,

492
00:21:25,370 --> 00:21:30,730
and the success rate refers
to getting to the final--

493
00:21:30,730 --> 00:21:33,490
getting success on the action--
sorry, the task that the user

494
00:21:33,490 --> 00:21:35,650
had assigned in this
particular case.

495
00:21:35,650 --> 00:21:38,410
So ReAct generally
outperforms what

496
00:21:38,410 --> 00:21:41,410
was possible with just
imitation learning

497
00:21:41,410 --> 00:21:44,490
or supervised
fine-tuning before.

498
00:21:44,490 --> 00:21:47,510
But it's still far below
