672
00:29:28,890 --> 00:29:31,970
They're using both training time
and inference-time execution

673
00:29:31,970 --> 00:29:32,890
feedback.

674
00:29:32,890 --> 00:29:36,457
So let me explain
the training time

675
00:29:36,457 --> 00:29:37,790
and the inference-time feedback.

676
00:29:37,790 --> 00:29:40,207
So at the very top in the
purple block, what you're seeing

677
00:29:40,207 --> 00:29:43,590
is that the model gets a natural
language problem description.

678
00:29:43,590 --> 00:29:46,290
For example, someone asked you
to, say, write a Hello World

679
00:29:46,290 --> 00:29:47,190
program.

680
00:29:47,190 --> 00:29:49,090
Then it generates
a code solution,

681
00:29:49,090 --> 00:29:52,490
which then evaluates on
some public set of tests.

682
00:29:52,490 --> 00:29:56,010
If the code fails, the execution
feedback is provided to the LLM

683
00:29:56,010 --> 00:29:57,770
for another attempt,
and this cycle

684
00:29:57,770 --> 00:30:01,170
will continue until
either the code will pass

685
00:30:01,170 --> 00:30:03,370
or it will reach a limit
of the number of turns it's

686
00:30:03,370 --> 00:30:06,730
supposed to do this loop for.

687
00:30:06,730 --> 00:30:10,530
And then based on this,
whatever set of solutions

688
00:30:10,530 --> 00:30:12,670
it has generated for
passing solutions,

689
00:30:12,670 --> 00:30:16,710
a private set of tests will
determine what reward to give,

690
00:30:16,710 --> 00:30:22,110
and then that will be used in
the PPO training loop with RL.

691
00:30:22,110 --> 00:30:24,310
So what here is
happening is there

692
00:30:24,310 --> 00:30:25,930
are two fundamental phases.

693
00:30:25,930 --> 00:30:28,630
This is the exploitation phase
of RL, where it's basically

694
00:30:28,630 --> 00:30:31,950
exploiting the current policy
through inference-time feedback

695
00:30:31,950 --> 00:30:34,850
loops, like whatever
policy model you have got.

696
00:30:34,850 --> 00:30:36,510
And on the right
side, you have update

697
00:30:36,510 --> 00:30:38,990
of the policy based on
the execution results,

698
00:30:38,990 --> 00:30:41,910
and this allows continuous
improvement for the model

699
00:30:41,910 --> 00:30:44,270
to self-improve here.

700
00:30:44,270 --> 00:30:48,190
So let's take a look
at how this works.

701
00:30:48,190 --> 00:30:49,910
You're given a
simple example where

702
00:30:49,910 --> 00:30:53,830
you're creating code to detect,
say, palindrome substrings.

703
00:30:53,830 --> 00:30:57,630
So that's what the problem
statement on the top is.

704
00:30:57,630 --> 00:31:00,250
In the first turn, it
generates a basic solution,

705
00:31:00,250 --> 00:31:03,230
but the implementation actually
fails in the public tests,

706
00:31:03,230 --> 00:31:08,990
and there's execution timeout,
which is a very common issue.

707
00:31:08,990 --> 00:31:12,270
After receiving this
feedback, the model actually

708
00:31:12,270 --> 00:31:16,350
generates a better solution
