00:44:53,130 --> 00:44:54,210
Yes.

997
00:44:54,210 --> 00:45:00,250
I think he would make this the
code of each element [INAUDIBLE]

998
00:45:00,250 --> 00:45:03,770
for summary so that [INAUDIBLE]
be something [INAUDIBLE],

999
00:45:03,770 --> 00:45:07,450
and then together, they can
fit in the comments window.

1000
00:45:10,162 --> 00:45:12,410
Any other ideas?

1001
00:45:12,410 --> 00:45:13,330
Yes.

1002
00:45:13,330 --> 00:45:17,030
I don't know if this applies
here, but in a regular setting,

1003
00:45:17,030 --> 00:45:20,650
I feel like you can build
a graph-regularized version

1004
00:45:20,650 --> 00:45:24,250
of your code base and
then do a similarity based

1005
00:45:24,250 --> 00:45:27,290
on what you can find
them, and again, do

1006
00:45:27,290 --> 00:45:30,510
another iteration on that,
just simple [INAUDIBLE].

1007
00:45:30,510 --> 00:45:34,050
And then you can
have in the loop what

1008
00:45:34,050 --> 00:45:37,430
you said about verifying and see
if you have enough information.

1009
00:45:37,430 --> 00:45:41,230
And then, if not, you can do
that, because in that case,

1010
00:45:41,230 --> 00:45:42,935
you can use the summarization.

1011
00:45:42,935 --> 00:45:44,810
You can use the things
that you're extracting

1012
00:45:44,810 --> 00:45:46,470
and then come up
with the answer.

1013
00:45:49,270 --> 00:45:51,630
So this is actually
the kind of thing

1014
00:45:51,630 --> 00:45:53,370
that actually runs
in Cloud Code.

1015
00:45:53,370 --> 00:45:56,110
It has to go search
for what is relevant

1016
00:45:56,110 --> 00:45:58,710
and then actually apply
the code, and it's there.

1017
00:45:58,710 --> 00:46:02,590
And one of the benchmarks that
targets this is like SWE-bench.

1018
00:46:02,590 --> 00:46:04,750
So all the ideas that
you folks have proposed

1019
00:46:04,750 --> 00:46:08,230
in the realm of searching for
something, having some summary,

1020
00:46:08,230 --> 00:46:10,710
doing some representation,
all of those

1021
00:46:10,710 --> 00:46:13,830
are perhaps the first step
to solving that problem

1022
00:46:13,830 --> 00:46:18,390
before you now do the test,
what code patch to apply,

1023
00:46:18,390 --> 00:46:21,150
and then whether it
has passed or not.

1024
00:46:21,150 --> 00:46:23,790
So Code Monks paper
from Azalea's lab

1025
00:46:23,790 --> 00:46:26,410
also targets very
similar ideas here.

1026
00:46:30,770 --> 00:46:32,210
Let's move to the last topic.

1027
00:46:32,210 --> 00:46:34,130
The last topic is
constitutional AI,

1028
00:46:34,130 --> 00:46:37,730
where we'll have the model
learn from AI feedback.

1029
00:46:37,730 --> 00:46:39,750
And the thing that
we are improving

1030
00:46:39,750 --> 00:46:42,430
about the model that
is harmlessness,

1031
00:46:42,430 --> 00:46:46,090
or its ability to
generate harmless outputs.

1032
00:46:46,090 --> 00:46:50,290
So we want it to be helpful, but
we also want it to be harmless.

1033
00:46:50,290 --> 00:46:53,770
So what happened with LLMs
when they are base models

1034
00:46:53,770 --> 00:46:57,450
is that you can give them
feedback in a lot of ways.

1035
00:46:57,450 --> 00:46:59,290
A typical way to
give feedback when

1036
00:46:59,290 --> 00:47:00,850
you are trying to
build a chatbot

1037
00:47:00,850 --> 00:47:02,650
would be that you
look at the outputs,

1038
00:47:02,650 --> 00:47:06,310
and you ask humans to rank them
in, is the model output correct?

1039
00:47:06,310 --> 00:47:08,230
So you show it two
different outputs.

1040
00:47:08,230 --> 00:47:11,210
You ask it whether it's
correct, whether it's useful,

1041
00:47:11,210 --> 00:47:12,970
and whether it's
specific enough.

1042
00:47:12,970 --> 00:47:15,500
And then based on those
human preferences,

1043
00:47:15,500 --> 00:47:17,250
you can build a reward
model, and then you

1044
00:47:17,250 --> 00:47:18,990
can use that to hillclimb.

1045
00:47:18,990 --> 00:47:22,450
So here is a Python
fine-tuning with RLHF

1046
00:47:22,450 --> 00:47:24,550
versus just Python fine tuning.

1047
00:47:24,550 --> 00:47:26,090
And as you increase
the model size,

1048
00:47:26,090 --> 00:47:29,090
you clearly see better
responses with RLHF.

1049
00:47:29,090 --> 00:47:31,890
So basically, that's
this whole notion of you

1050
00:47:31,890 --> 00:47:36,290
can use these
preference responses

1051
00:47:36,290 --> 00:47:40,490
from humans as human feedback
to hillclimb on the model.

1052
00:47:40,490 --> 00:47:42,050
Now, that doesn't
scale very well,

1053
00:47:42,050 --> 00:47:44,450
because if you have to
collect tens of thousands

1054
00:47:44,450 --> 00:47:47,130
of human labels,
that's extremely

1055
00:47:47,130 --> 00:47:49,030
time consuming and tedious.

1056
00:47:49,030 --> 00:47:51,210
Imagine that you generate
all these model outputs,

1057
00:47:51,210 --> 00:47:53,270
then you have to ask
humans to rate them.

1058
00:47:53,270 --> 00:47:56,090
So one of the very
interesting principles,

1059
00:47:56,090 --> 00:47:57,770
given that the
models can reason,

1060
00:47:57,770 --> 00:48:00,660
was to use this notion
of constitutional

1061
00:48:00,660 --> 00:48:02,910
that Anthropic came up with,
which was very much based

1062
00:48:02,910 --> 00:48:04,450
on human-written principles.

1063
00:48:04,450 --> 00:48:06,790
So they basically
used constitutional AI

1064
00:48:06,790 --> 00:48:09,090
or human-written principles
called the Constitution,

1065
00:48:09,090 --> 00:48:12,230
and then used them to improve
the model by describing

1066
00:48:12,230 --> 00:48:14,070
what is the desired behavior.

1067
00:48:14,070 --> 00:48:15,750
So humans don't need
to be in the loop,

1068
00:48:15,750 --> 00:48:17,610
except to write
that Constitution.

1069
00:48:17,610 --> 00:48:19,590
And the reason this
notion of rules works

1070
00:48:19,590 --> 00:48:22,550
is because the models get
better at instruction following.

1071
00:48:22,550 --> 00:48:25,230
So if you ask the
model to format

1072
00:48:25,230 --> 00:48:27,270
the response in a
certain way, the model

1073
00:48:27,270 --> 00:48:28,810
is able to follow
that instruction.

1074
00:48:28,810 --> 00:48:31,950
Or if you ask the model
that, does this response have

1075
00:48:31,950 --> 00:48:34,030
this particular
behavior, then the model

1076
00:48:34,030 --> 00:48:37,610
is able to actually answer
that in truthful ways.

1077
00:48:37,610 --> 00:48:39,750
That's what the
constitutional AI

1078
00:48:39,750 --> 00:48:42,590
aspect will be able to exploit.

1079
00:48:42,590 --> 00:48:44,210
So what constitutional
AI did was

1080
00:48:44,210 --> 00:48:46,490
it came up with a set
of 16 principles, which

1081
00:48:46,490 --> 00:48:50,490
they call Constitution, and
those will define the model

1082
00:48:50,490 --> 00:48:52,168
behavior, and they use that.

1083
00:48:52,168 --> 00:48:54,210
So these are going to be
the set of prompts which

1084
00:48:54,210 --> 00:48:56,930
will elicit whether it's
following the Constitution

1085
00:48:56,930 --> 00:49:00,270
or not, and then use that to
critique the model itself.

1086
00:49:00,270 --> 00:49:01,690
I'll show you an example.

1087
00:49:01,690 --> 00:49:04,150
And then ask you to
revise its response.

1088
00:49:04,150 --> 00:49:06,250
So you're basically
saying whether--

1089
00:49:06,250 --> 00:49:09,452
we're basically using
some red-teaming prompts.

1090
00:49:09,452 --> 00:49:10,910
You're looking at
the model output.

1091
00:49:10,910 --> 00:49:11,785
You're critiquing it.

1092
00:49:11,785 --> 00:49:13,690
You're getting a
revision, and then you're

1093
00:49:13,690 --> 00:49:15,690
using that to
fine-tune the model.

1094
00:49:15,690 --> 00:49:18,050
And so that's the
supervised fine-tuning stage

1095
00:49:18,050 --> 00:49:20,970
for constitutional AI.

1096
00:49:20,970 --> 00:49:24,210
And then this is the RL stage
where you're basically doing

1097
00:49:24,210 --> 00:49:25,430
the same set of responses.

1098
00:49:25,430 --> 00:49:28,470
And then using AI feedback
to train a preference model,

1099
00:49:28,470 --> 00:49:31,790
and then using that to
train the final model.

1100
00:49:31,790 --> 00:49:34,610
I'll show that in
detail in just a second.

1101
00:49:34,610 --> 00:49:37,410
So before we go there,
let's take a look

1102
00:49:37,410 --> 00:49:40,410
at what might be an
example Constitution.

1103
00:49:40,410 --> 00:49:41,950
I will not read
this word by word,

1104
00:49:41,950 --> 00:49:43,990
but I'll show you
the basic examples.

1105
00:49:43,990 --> 00:49:48,350
So the critique request in
this particular box diagram

1106
00:49:48,350 --> 00:49:50,710
that I showed you is that
there is a red-teaming prompt.

1107
00:49:50,710 --> 00:49:52,050
The model has an output.

1108
00:49:52,050 --> 00:49:54,030
And then the critique
request is basically

1109
00:49:54,030 --> 00:49:58,630
asking if the model output has
something harmful or unethical

1110
00:49:58,630 --> 00:49:59,630
and so on.

1111
00:49:59,630 --> 00:50:01,230
And the revision
request is basically

1112
00:50:01,230 --> 00:50:04,470
asking you to remove
any of those responses.

1113
00:50:04,470 --> 00:50:06,150
A second critique
request might be that,

1114
00:50:06,150 --> 00:50:08,350
does it have any gender bias?

1115
00:50:08,350 --> 00:50:12,690
And the argument about why that
might be having a gender bias.

1116
00:50:12,690 --> 00:50:14,110
And then the
revision request is,

1117
00:50:14,110 --> 00:50:16,230
can you remove
any trace of that?

1118
00:50:16,230 --> 00:50:18,270
And the third one
is more like, is it

1119
00:50:18,270 --> 00:50:20,910
inappropriate for
young children?

1120
00:50:20,910 --> 00:50:24,210
And then what would it take
for it to be appropriate?

1121
00:50:24,210 --> 00:50:26,670
And then the revision
request is rewrite it.

1122
00:50:26,670 --> 00:50:28,750
So if you look at
each of these, it

1123
00:50:28,750 --> 00:50:31,888
requires the model to be able
to identify these behaviors.

1124
00:50:31,888 --> 00:50:33,430
And then the second
thing it requires

1125
00:50:33,430 --> 00:50:36,510
is to be able to follow the
instruction of now rewrite it

1126
00:50:36,510 --> 00:50:43,050
with this particular style or
way, or removing this content.

1127
00:50:43,050 --> 00:50:46,890
And then the way this loop works
is that humans come in and set

1128
00:50:46,890 --> 00:50:51,170
right a set of principles
that will be used

1129
00:50:51,170 --> 00:50:52,770
for the self-improvement loop.

1130
00:50:52,770 --> 00:50:54,267
In the supervised
learning stage,

1131
00:50:54,267 --> 00:50:55,850
you're basically
fine-tuning the model

1132
00:50:55,850 --> 00:50:58,970
on this data generated by the
self-critique and revision

1133
00:50:58,970 --> 00:50:59,670
stage.

1134
00:50:59,670 --> 00:51:01,810
So the critique request,
as I showed you,

1135
00:51:01,810 --> 00:51:04,950
might be something harmful,
something unethical,

1136
00:51:04,950 --> 00:51:06,890
and the revision might
be, OK, remove anything

1137
00:51:06,890 --> 00:51:10,810
that's harmful and unethical.

1138
00:51:10,810 --> 00:51:14,870
And just with supervised
learning with a number of turns,

1139
00:51:14,870 --> 00:51:16,730
you are able to--

1140
00:51:16,730 --> 00:51:18,150
just with the
number of revisions,

1141
00:51:18,150 --> 00:51:22,170
the harmlessness improves
and the helpfulness

1142
00:51:22,170 --> 00:51:24,170
will decline if you
just keep increasing

1143
00:51:24,170 --> 00:51:25,670
in supervised fine-tuning.

1144
00:51:25,670 --> 00:51:29,130
But overall, the helpfulness
plus harmlessness

1145
00:51:29,130 --> 00:51:32,090
will improve monotonically,
because if you're

1146
00:51:32,090 --> 00:51:36,417
trying to get the model to a
certain set of fixed principles,

1147
00:51:36,417 --> 00:51:38,750
then it does become less
helpful is what they're saying.

1148
00:51:38,750 --> 00:51:41,950
But overall, it's more
helpful and more harmless

1149
00:51:41,950 --> 00:51:44,310
together is their claim.

1150
00:51:44,310 --> 00:51:46,770
And then in terms of the
reinforcement learning stage,

1151
00:51:46,770 --> 00:51:48,310
first, they train
a preference model

1152
00:51:48,310 --> 00:51:52,570
based on the responses in
step 1 and the Constitution,

1153
00:51:52,570 --> 00:51:56,230
and then they fine-tune the LLM
to maximize over this preference

1154
00:51:56,230 --> 00:51:56,950
model.

1155
00:51:56,950 --> 00:51:59,870
So they're basically
getting the model

1156
00:51:59,870 --> 00:52:03,150
to respond in a way that's
most thoughtful, respectful,

1157
00:52:03,150 --> 00:52:03,850
and cordial.

1158
00:52:03,850 --> 00:52:05,368
So this is basically the loop.

1159
00:52:05,368 --> 00:52:07,910
So there's a preference model
that's being trained separately

1160
00:52:07,910 --> 00:52:09,750
with this Constitution,
and then that's

1161
00:52:09,750 --> 00:52:13,210
what is being used to fine-tune
the LLM to get better.

1162
00:52:13,210 --> 00:52:16,070
So instead of the
RLHF loop where

1163
00:52:16,070 --> 00:52:18,008
you had a lot of human
feedback, now you're

1164
00:52:18,008 --> 00:52:19,550
using this preference
model, which is

1165
00:52:19,550 --> 00:52:21,670
trained with the Constitution.

1166
00:52:21,670 --> 00:52:22,750
Yes.

1167
00:52:22,750 --> 00:52:26,350
So all constitutions, maybe
there may be some amendments.

1168
00:52:26,350 --> 00:52:30,790
If so, how do you make sure that
you're cost efficiently updating

1169
00:52:30,790 --> 00:52:33,470
the constitutions for
this model without having

1170
00:52:33,470 --> 00:52:35,490
to train all over again?

1171
00:52:35,490 --> 00:52:39,070
And if so, how do you make sure
that the previous rules are

1172
00:52:39,070 --> 00:52:41,050
removed completely?

1173
00:52:41,050 --> 00:52:43,110
So there's two answers
to your question.

1174
00:52:43,110 --> 00:52:47,330
So one answer is that
[? AI ?] is generally done

1175
00:52:47,330 --> 00:52:50,210
as the last stage of model.

1176
00:52:50,210 --> 00:52:55,150
And typically, once you have
a pretrained base model,

1177
00:52:55,150 --> 00:52:58,410
post-training has typically
been a much smaller percentage

1178
00:52:58,410 --> 00:53:02,450
of compute, so maybe 5%, and
that happens fairly frequently

1179
00:53:02,450 --> 00:53:04,090
for models to get updated.

1180
00:53:04,090 --> 00:53:06,530
So in that sense,
if you do think

1181
00:53:06,530 --> 00:53:09,290
that the Constitution
should be updated,

1182
00:53:09,290 --> 00:53:11,418
that happens at a
certain frequency.

1183
00:53:11,418 --> 00:53:13,210
Overall, I think the
question you're asking

1184
00:53:13,210 --> 00:53:14,830
is this notion of
continual learning,

1185
00:53:14,830 --> 00:53:17,110
and how do we get the models
to adhere to certain behavior.

1186
00:53:17,110 --> 00:53:19,735
I think that's an open research
problem at this point in time--

1187
00:53:19,735 --> 00:53:22,650
how do we get the models to
forget certain set of knowledge

1188
00:53:22,650 --> 00:53:24,650
or to adhere to a
new set of knowledge

1189
00:53:24,650 --> 00:53:26,850
or to follow a new set of rules?

1190
00:53:26,850 --> 00:53:28,390
So that's definitely something.

1191
00:53:28,390 --> 00:53:30,730
There are interesting
answers to that problem where

1192
00:53:30,730 --> 00:53:33,065
you can do some
canceling of getting

1193
00:53:33,065 --> 00:53:34,690
it to forget a certain
set of knowledge

1194
00:53:34,690 --> 00:53:37,258
by looking at, say,
interpretability methods

1195
00:53:37,258 --> 00:53:39,300
and so on of like, can
you cancel that knowledge?

1196
00:53:39,300 --> 00:53:42,080
But then it's not
proven that you can get

1197
00:53:42,080 --> 00:53:43,540
the models to forget something.

1198
00:53:49,680 --> 00:53:52,657
So in terms of how
this scales, so this

1199
00:53:52,657 --> 00:53:54,740
is the number of training
sequences they're using.

1200
00:53:54,740 --> 00:53:56,157
And on the x-axis,
they're showing

1201
00:53:56,157 --> 00:53:58,760
the score of helpfulness Elo.

1202
00:53:58,760 --> 00:54:02,060
Elo means that humans prefer
it in terms of helpfulness.

1203
00:54:02,060 --> 00:54:04,860
And then, similarly,
on the right side,

1204
00:54:04,860 --> 00:54:09,040
they're showing harmlessness
Elo where the humans prefer it

1205
00:54:09,040 --> 00:54:10,920
in terms of it
being more harmless.

1206
00:54:10,920 --> 00:54:14,120
So now that we have replaced
human preferences with AI

1207
00:54:14,120 --> 00:54:17,000
feedback, we are
comparing it to, say,

1208
00:54:17,000 --> 00:54:19,680
just using
helpfulness-based RLHF

1209
00:54:19,680 --> 00:54:21,640
or
helpful-plus-harmlessness-based

1210
00:54:21,640 --> 00:54:24,040
RLHF, which is all
human feedback-based,

1211
00:54:24,040 --> 00:54:25,920
and then just using
constitutional AI

1212
00:54:25,920 --> 00:54:28,280
and constitutional AI
with Chain of Thought.

1213
00:54:28,280 --> 00:54:31,360
And what they roughly show is
that just getting the model

1214
00:54:31,360 --> 00:54:34,560
to evaluate its responses
based on these principles,

1215
00:54:34,560 --> 00:54:38,540
you still get the model to
be equally helpful, maybe

1216
00:54:38,540 --> 00:54:40,140
a little bit less
helpful, but it's

1217
00:54:40,140 --> 00:54:43,700
much less harmless as a result.
So the harmlessness scores

1218
00:54:43,700 --> 00:54:46,260
are much higher, and
that was something

1219
00:54:46,260 --> 00:54:51,220
that the Claude models
were extremely strong at.

1220
00:54:51,220 --> 00:54:53,380
And now that's a set
of techniques that

1221
00:54:53,380 --> 00:54:55,700
are used across the models.

1222
00:54:55,700 --> 00:54:56,260
Yes.

1223
00:54:56,260 --> 00:55:00,020
The Chain of Thought has
lower helpfulness Elo.

1224
00:55:00,020 --> 00:55:01,660
Say that again.

1225
00:55:01,660 --> 00:55:06,500
The Chain of Thought has
lower helpfulness Elo.

1226
00:55:06,500 --> 00:55:07,300
Yes.

1227
00:55:07,300 --> 00:55:11,060
Results [INAUDIBLE].

1228
00:55:11,060 --> 00:55:13,500
So it means that Chain of
Thought actually worse,

1229
00:55:13,500 --> 00:55:15,340
the performance.

1230
00:55:15,340 --> 00:55:16,860
Possibly.

1231
00:55:16,860 --> 00:55:18,300
That's a good point.

1232
00:55:18,300 --> 00:55:20,000
Is there a measure?

1233
00:55:20,000 --> 00:55:23,580
And interestingly is that,
