1317
00:58:58,040 --> 00:58:59,580
But then, if you
do Constitutional

1318
00:58:59,580 --> 00:59:02,840
supervised learning, then you're
still worse than doing RLHF.

1319
00:59:02,840 --> 00:59:05,540
But when you do Constitutional
RL with Chain of Thought,

1320
00:59:05,540 --> 00:59:09,260
that's where you get the
frontier in terms of the best

1321
00:59:09,260 --> 00:59:11,940
Pareto frontier in terms of
tradeoff between harmlessness

1322
00:59:11,940 --> 00:59:12,760
and helpfulness.

1323
00:59:12,760 --> 00:59:16,020
And that's one of the key
ideas of this paper as to you

1324
00:59:16,020 --> 00:59:17,780
can strike the right
Pareto frontier

1325
00:59:17,780 --> 00:59:19,760
between helpfulness
and harmlessness

1326
00:59:19,760 --> 00:59:21,040
in this particular case.

1327
00:59:21,040 --> 00:59:23,642
If you were to generalize
these set of ideas across--

1328
00:59:23,642 --> 00:59:25,100
I mean, generally
anything you want

1329
00:59:25,100 --> 00:59:27,860
to do in instruction
following where

1330
00:59:27,860 --> 00:59:31,060
you want the model to follow
a subset of instructions

1331
00:59:31,060 --> 00:59:32,720
but they might be conflicting--

1332
00:59:32,720 --> 00:59:35,500
then this style of
principles applies.

1333
00:59:38,360 --> 00:59:42,120
And some follow-on work that
went and built on top of that

1334
00:59:42,120 --> 00:59:45,360
includes the fact that there
was another work that tried

1335
00:59:45,360 --> 00:59:48,080
to compare RLAIF with RLHF.

1336
00:59:48,080 --> 00:59:49,800
There was self-refine,
where it talked

1337
00:59:49,800 --> 00:59:52,320
about iterative refinements
