1354
01:00:33,720 --> 01:00:36,340
So in terms of the
ReAct approach,

1355
01:00:36,340 --> 01:00:39,140
what we first looked
at was this notion of,

1356
01:00:39,140 --> 01:00:42,100
when we talk to LLMs, we can
get them to give model outputs

1357
01:00:42,100 --> 01:00:44,260
and what we wanted
these model outputs

1358
01:00:44,260 --> 01:00:48,560
to be preferable by humans,
which has been a focus.

1359
01:00:48,560 --> 01:00:51,580
But what ReAct starts to
look at is this notion of,

1360
01:00:51,580 --> 01:00:54,440
can we get the models
to act in real world?

1361
01:00:54,440 --> 01:00:57,440
And based on that
feedback and combining

1362
01:00:57,440 --> 01:00:58,940
that with reasoning,
we can get them

1363
01:00:58,940 --> 01:01:02,160
to be grounded so they can give
better answers to questions,

1364
01:01:02,160 --> 01:01:03,520
can check facts better.

1365
01:01:03,520 --> 01:01:08,260
Or if we put these LLMs as
decision makers in, say, gaming

1366
01:01:08,260 --> 01:01:11,720
environments or in other
environments related to, say,

1367
01:01:11,720 --> 01:01:14,860
web browser agents,
and so on, then they

1368
01:01:14,860 --> 01:01:16,580
can make useful decisions.

1369
01:01:16,580 --> 01:01:19,600
And one interesting aspect
of doing it in ReAct style,

1370
01:01:19,600 --> 01:01:22,820
in language space, ends up being
that the decision traces are

1371
01:01:22,820 --> 01:01:24,240
extremely interpretable.

1372
01:01:24,240 --> 01:01:26,100
There are several
works on top of that

1373
01:01:26,100 --> 01:01:29,400
try to combine this reasoning
and acting in different ways

1374
01:01:29,400 --> 01:01:32,640
and trying to get the models
to do better tool calling

1375
01:01:32,640 --> 01:01:35,360
to the point where
this tool calling is

1376
01:01:35,360 --> 01:01:39,120
